sentences.t:
μῆτερ ἐμή, δύσμητερ, ἀπηνέα θυμὸν ἔχουσα,
τίφθ’ οὕτω πατρὸς νοσφίζεαι, οὐδὲ παρ’ αὐτὸν
ἑζομένη μύθοισιν ἀνείρεαι οὐδὲ μεταλλᾷς;

sentences.tr:
mēter emē, dysmēter, apēnea thymon echousa,
tiphth’ houtō patros nosphizeai, oude par’ auton
hezomenē mythoisin aneireai oude metallāis;

sentences.l:
Truly no other woman, at least, so, with enduring heart,
would stand aloof from her husband, who, for him, many evils having toiled through,
should come in the twentieth year to his fatherland;

But if truly indeed
it is Odysseus, and he comes to his house, then very surely we two
shall know each other, and better;

For indeed anyone, one man having killed in the land,
for whom not many may be helpers hereafter,
flees, both kinsmen-by-marriage leaving and fatherland;

And then the divine minstrel, holding the clear-sounding lyre,
let him lead for us the play-loving dance,
so that someone might say there is a wedding, hearing it from outside,
either going along the road, or one of those who dwell around;

lest earlier a wide fame of the slaughter throughout the town arise
of the men, the suitors, before we have gone out
to our own farm, rich in trees;

So he spoke, and they then readily heard him and obeyed,
first, then, they washed and put on tunics,
and the women were made ready;

But Odysseus, great-spirited, in his own house
Eurynome the housekeeper washed and anointed with oil,
and about him a fine cloak threw, and a tunic;

Truly no other woman, at least, so, with enduring heart,
would stand aloof from her husband, who, for him, many evils having toiled through,
should come in the twentieth year to his fatherland;
